    "body":  "## Overview\n\nAllocate HF3FS metadata pages only for distinct cache misses. A mixed batch containing an already complete key must not fail when the remaining pages are sufficient for its new keys.\n\nRefs vllm-project/vllm#62389. This addresses the near-capacity allocation subproblem; it does **not** implement eviction or resolve exhaustion of a genuinely full pool. Please keep the issue open.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Existing complete keys reuse their current pages without contributing to the new-page budget.\n- Duplicate new keys in one metadata allocation call consume one page, avoiding an unreachable allocation hidden by the key-based response.\n- If the distinct misses do not fit, they still fail together; existing hits and the free pool are preserved.\n\n## Validation\n\nBase: `43caf5b3520b704fa766f00fc2b24da25c78e084`, Python 3.12.3.\n\n| Local validation | Unmodified base + new tests | Patched |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Existing metadata test file + 4 new cases | 3 failed, 15 passed | 18 passed |\n| Real localhost HTTP server/client, 2 pages, first confirm one key, then allocate cached + new | Not run as a baseline HTTP arm | Both receive distinct valid pages; confirm and look up both successfully |\n| Controlled real-engine save, 1 cached key + 1 new key, 1 remaining page | Save fails | Both pages saved; a repeated request loads both blocks |\n\nThe CPU runner imports the complete metadata source module, copies the upstream test file, and changes only its import to a standalone module name. It disables framework `conftest.py` to avoid unrelated GPU-platform initialization. These results are isolated metadata tests, **not** a complete vLLM checkout test run. The HTTP probe uses the actual FastAPI app, uvicorn and `Hf3fsGlobalMetadataClient`, with no mocked state methods.\n\nReproduction driver and logs are retained in the local project review packet. Intended checkout command for human validation:\n\n```bash\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/v1/kv_connector/unit/test_hf3fs_metadata_server.py -q\n```\n\nServing smoke test: RTX 5060 Laptop 8 GiB, installed vLLM 0.31.0, Qwen3-0.6B BF16, MRV1 eager, TP1, async scheduling and local prefix caching disabled, existing HF3FS file fallback. Each arm uses the pinned-main metadata module in a local HTTP server. The harness advertises two pages and forces the second scheduler lookup to miss, simulating a lookup made before a concurrent prefix save completes. The actual connector then saves `[cached, new]`. Baseline fails the save, patched succeeds. The patched repeated request reports 32 external hit tokens and loads 2 blocks. The 4 greedy tokens match its cold request and the baseline cold request. One run per arm; this is a controlled serving smoke test, **not** a quality evaluation, a full latest-main GPU test, a real HF3FS cluster, TP/multi-instance concurrency validation or a performance benchmark.\n\nThe submitting human has confirmed line-by-line review of this patch and a personal run of the relevant tests.\n\n## Details\n\nPreviously the allocator reserved `len(keys)` pages before discovering complete cache hits. With two pages, one confirmed key and one free page, requesting `[cached, new]` asks for two new pages. The all-or-nothing primitive returns none, so the new key fails despite having enough space. Resolving hits first makes the reservation size equal to the number of distinct new keys; the response retains the original key order and format.\n\nAll changes remain under the existing metadata lock. There is no page recycling, ownership transfer, connector protocol change or additional device synchronization. Incomplete multi-rank keys retain the existing treatment.\n\nDuplicate-work audit on 2026-10-19: the issue had no comments/assignees; open PR search for `62389 in:body` returned zero and the HF3FS area search returned 17 PRs. None found implements miss-only metadata allocation. In particular #61248 drains/validates background save writers and #61247 updates resumed block tables; this patch changes neither path. Search coverage cannot establish absence of private work.\n\nOpenAI Codex assisted with investigation, implementation, tests and this draft. The submitting human has confirmed line-by-line review and a personal run of the relevant tests.\n\n---\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003ePull Request Checklist\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n- [x] I used vLLM\u0027s `/pr-checklist` skill.\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Metadata-local change preserving response format and miss-batch failure semantics.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** CPU metadata, HTTP and a controlled file-backed GPU serving smoke test pass; the submitting human confirmed a personal test run.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Python-applicable upstream Ruff, typos and local hooks pass, including mypy and CI tethering; human review confirmed.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Scoped claims, related work, root cause and limitations are explicit.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n",
